1
00:00:00,036 --> 00:00:13,696
OFFCUT turns leftover paper into a collage you can actually make. This is an engineering demonstration using the built-in illustrated example.

2
00:00:14,579 --> 00:00:26,649
We load the illustrated example, not a camera photo. The same real image AI will segment it in the browser.

3
00:00:27,466 --> 00:00:38,826
Confirming the A4 corners runs real local segmentation. Four scraps are detected automatically.

4
00:00:39,668 --> 00:00:51,428
The material map shows the learned masks on the corrected sheet. Every boundary must be reviewed before cutting.

5
00:00:52,315 --> 00:01:07,575
OFFCUT requires a mandatory human boundary review before cutting. Checking the review box is only a recording step and is not evidence that a human inspected each boundary.

6
00:01:08,468 --> 00:01:19,338
Choosing Heart and making the collage assigns thirty-six unique crops from the illustrated input.

7
00:01:20,143 --> 00:01:31,403
Clicking a piece traces it back to its source on the original sheet. No piece is invented or reused.

8
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:43,200
Hiding the piece numbers shows the clean mosaic. Each square is a real crop, not a recolour.

9
00:01:44,076 --> 00:01:56,856
Remove one paper and the collage recomputes from what remains. Every tile still comes from a unique surviving source.

10
00:01:57,661 --> 00:02:10,001
The cutting and assembly plan downloads with a twenty-millimetre calibration square. Physical cutting remains unverified.

11
00:02:10,824 --> 00:02:21,594
Built by Rishik Rontala. Physical cutting and real success are still unverified.
